tel 1.0

base gesticulate
import soundness

info   media types as structured values, checked against the IANA registry as the code compiles, and multipart bodies
detail
    _Gesticulate_ represents a [media type](https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/) —
    `application/json`, `text/html; charset=UTF-8`, `image/svg+xml` — as a `MediaType` with a
    typed group, subtype, suffixes and parameters, in place of a string whose misspelling a
    browser silently mishandles. The `media"…"` interpolator checks a literal against a copy of
    IANA's registry while the code compiles, so `media"application/jsom"` is a compile error
    with the registered name it probably meant; text met at runtime is parsed with
    `as[MediaType]`, which raises `MediaType.Error` naming what was wrong.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val json: MediaType = media"application/json"
    val html: MediaType = media"text/html"(charset = t"UTF-8")
    html.show                                            // t"text/html; charset=UTF-8"
    t"image/svg+xml".as[MediaType].suffixes              // List(Media.Suffix.Xml)
    t"application/json; charset=UTF-8".as[MediaType].at(t"charset")   // t"UTF-8"
    ```

    Around the type are three further pieces. The `Media` typeclass gives a *type* its media
    type — text is `text/plain`, anything with a filename guesses from its extension through
    `Extensions` — which is how an HTTP response, a served file or an attachment labels itself
    without re-stating what it is, and `ascribe` pairs any byte source with an explicit media
    type as a `Content`. The `Construable` typeclass maps a media type, as a singleton string,
    to the Scala type a body of that type is read as and written from, so that an HTTP client
    can construe a response by its `Content-Type`: each mapping is a given in the
    `construables` choice package, imported by name. And `Multipart` parses the
    `multipart/form-data` format of file uploads into typed `Multipart.Part`s, incrementally, each
    part's body lent from the input as it is read.

    The library is one source set, `gesticulate.core`, which compiles for the JVM, Scala.js
    and Scala Native; the registry the `media"…"` check consults is compiled into the library
    at build time, so the check needs no network and no classpath resource. Everything is
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `construables` choice package merges with
    the umbrella's own, which gathers the mappings every format library declares —
    `jsonConstruable`, `xmlConstruable`, `pngConstruable`, … — so a user writes
    `import soundness.*` and then imports the construables they want by name.

refer gesticulate.MediaType
refer gesticulate.Media
refer gesticulate.Multipart
refer gesticulate.Construable

entry .MediaType   a media type as a value: its group, subtype, suffixes and parameters
  detail
      A `MediaType` is the four parts RFC 2046 defines, each typed: `#group` is one of the
      eleven top-level types (`Media.Group`), `#subtype` is registered, vendor, personal or
      experimental (`Media.Subtype`), `#suffixes` are the `+xml`, `+json`, `+zip` structured
      syntax suffixes (`Media.Suffix`), and `#parameters` are the `key=value` pairs, of which
      `charset` is much the most common. It is written as `media"…"`, checked as the code
      compiles, or parsed from text with `as[MediaType]` (the `.decodable` given) or matched
      with `.unapply`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val mediaType = t"application/json; charset=UTF-8".as[MediaType]
      mediaType.group               // Media.Group.Application
      mediaType.subtype             // Media.Subtype.Standard(t"json")
      mediaType.basic               // t"application/json"
      mediaType.base                // media"application/json"
      mediaType.at(t"charset")      // t"UTF-8"
      media"text/html"(charset = t"UTF-8").show   // t"text/html; charset=UTF-8"
      ```

      A media type shows as it is written on the wire (`.showable`, `.encodable`), with each
      parameter as `; key=value`, and inspects as the source which would make it,
      `media"text/html; charset=UTF-8"`. Two media types are equal when their parts are, so a
      `Content-Type` with parameters compares unequal to its `media"…"` literal: compare `#base`
      or `#basic` when the parameters do not matter.
  refer gesticulate.media
  refer gesticulate.Media
  refer gesticulate.Extensions
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering as the literal which would make it: `media"text/html"`
  entry .showable   renders a media type as it is written: `type/subtype+suffix; key=value`
      The group and subtype in lower case, each suffix with its `+`, then each parameter as
      `; key=value`. What `.encodable` and the `inspectable` rendering are built on, and what
      a `Content-Type` header carries: a media type with a `charset` or `boundary` parameter
      keeps it here, where `#basic` would drop it.
  entry .encodable   `MediaType is Encodable in Text`, as `.showable` renders it
  entry .decodable   `text.as[MediaType]`: parses text as a media type, raising `MediaType.Error`
      The given which lets any text be decoded as a `MediaType`, through `Media.parse`. It
      needs a `Tactic[MediaType.Error]` in scope, which it retains, so the instance is typed
      `(MediaType is Decodable in Text)^{tactic}`. The parse accepts the registered, `vnd.`,
      `prs.`, `x-` and `x.` subtype forms, hyphenated suffixes (`json-seq`), and parameters
      separated by `;` with surrounding whitespace trimmed, and does not consult the registry:
      an unregistered standard subtype parses, where the `media"…"` literal would not compile.
  entry .unapply   matches text which parses as a media type, as an `Option`
      `MediaType.unapply(t"text/plain")` is `Some(mediaType)`, and `None` for text which is
      not a media type, so `case MediaType(mediaType) =>` matches a header's value without a
      tactic in scope.
  entry .interpolable   the given behind the `media"…"` interpolator
      The `Interpolable` whose `interpolate` is the macro: it validates the one literal part
      against the registry and the parser at expansion time and splices the parsed value. A
      user writes `media"…"` rather than touching this.
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a media type
    detail
        Error 353: _the value `value` is not a valid media type; `reason`_. Raised through a
        `Tactic[MediaType.Error]` by `Media.parse`, and so by `as[MediaType]`, and reported as
        a compile error by `media"…"`. The `.Reason` says what was wrong, and its number is the
        error's sub-number (353.1 to 353.5).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        capture[MediaType.Error](t"applicationjson".as[MediaType]).reason
        // MediaType.Error.Reason.NotOneSlash
        ```
    entry .Reason   why text was not a media type
      detail
          Each has a `number` and a `message`:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `NotOneSlash`  1  _a media type should always contain exactly one '/' character_
          row  `MissingParam`  2  _a terminal ';' suggests that a parameter is missing_  also for a parameter with no `=`
          row  `InvalidGroup`  3  _the type must be one of: application, audio, … or haptics_
          row  `InvalidChar(char)`  4  _the character '`char`' is not allowed_  whitespace, a control character, or one of the `tspecials`
          row  `InvalidSuffix(suffix)`  5  _the suffix '`suffix`' is not recognized_  not one of `Media.Suffix`'s cases
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` to `5`, which is the error's sub-number
      entry #message   the reason as a sentence
    entry #value   the text which was not a media type
    entry #reason   why it was not
  entry #group   the top-level type: `Media.Group.Application`, `Text`, `Image`, …
      Called `group` rather than `type` only because `type` is a Scala keyword.
  entry #subtype   the subtype, as `Media.Subtype.Standard`, `Vendor`, `Personal` or `X`
      Which of the four trees a subtype is in is part of the value, not a matter of reading
      its prefix: `t"application/vnd.api+json".as[MediaType].subtype` is
      `Media.Subtype.Vendor(t"api")`.
  entry #suffixes   the structured syntax suffixes, in order, as `Media.Suffix`es
      `List(Media.Suffix.Xml)` for `image/svg+xml`; `Nil` for most media types. A suffix
      carries meaning — `svg+xml` is XML — which is why it is a typed part rather than a
      character of the subtype.
  entry #parameters   the `key=value` parameters, in order, as pairs of text
      `charset`, `boundary`, `version`, …. `#at` looks one up by name; `apply` with named
      arguments adds some.
  entry #basic   the media type without its parameters, as text: `application/json`
      The group, subtype and suffixes rendered; what a `Content-Type` is matched by when its
      `charset` or `boundary` should not take part in the comparison.
  entry #base   the media type without its parameters, as a `MediaType`
      `contentType.base == media"multipart/form-data"` is the comparison a request handler
      makes before reading the `boundary` parameter.
  entry #at   the value of a named parameter, or `Unset`
      `mediaType.at(t"charset")`; the first parameter of that name if there are several.
  entry #applyDynamicNamed   `mediaType(charset = t"UTF-8")`: the media type with parameters added
      The `Dynamic` application which lets parameters be written as named arguments:
      `media"text/html"(charset = t"UTF-8")`. Each argument's name is the parameter's key and
      its `Text` value the parameter's value, appended after any existing parameters.

entry .media   the `media"…"` interpolator: a media type literal, checked against the registry as the code compiles
    `media"application/json"` is a `MediaType`, checked while the code compiles: the group
    must be one of the eleven top-level types, the subtype must contain no forbidden
    character, and a subtype in the standard tree — not `vnd.`, `prs.`, `x-` or `x.` — must be
    in IANA's registry, or the literal is a compile error naming the nearest registered type,
    _application/jsom is not a registered media type; did you mean application/json?_. The
    suffixes and parameters are then parsed exactly as `as[MediaType]` parses them, so an
    unknown suffix or a dangling `;` is a compile error too.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    media"application/json"
    media"application/epub+zip"
    media"application/vnd.example.widget+json"   // vendor types are not looked up
    media"text/html"(charset = t"UTF-8")
    media"application/jsom"                      // does not compile
    ```

    The registry is a copy of IANA's published list compiled into the library at build time,
    so the check needs no network. The interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions, since the
    literal is checked at compile time; a media type known only at runtime is parsed with
    `as[MediaType]`, which does not consult the registry.

entry .Media   a type's own media type, and the grammar of media types: groups, subtypes, suffixes
  detail
      As a **typeclass**, `Media` gives a type its media type: a value which `is Media` has
      `#mediaType`, which is how an HTTP response, a served file or an attachment labels
      itself without anyone re-stating what it is. `Text` is `text/plain` (`.text`); anything
      `Nominable` — a file, a path, a named asset — guesses from the extension of its name
      (`.nominable`, through `Extensions.guess`); and the format libraries each declare their
      own (`Json is Media`, `Html is Media`, …) in their companions.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"hello".mediaType   // media"text/plain"

      case class Upload(name: Text)

      given Upload is Media:
        extension (upload: Upload) def mediaType: MediaType = media"application/octet-stream"
      ```

      As an **object**, `Media` holds the grammar: the enumerations `.Group`, `.Subtype` and
      `.Suffix` of which a `MediaType` is made, and `.parse`, the parser behind
      `as[MediaType]`, `MediaType.unapply` and `media"…"`.
  refer gesticulate.MediaType
  refer gesticulate.Extensions
  refer gesticulate.ascribe
  entry .text   `Text is Media`: `text/plain`
      In implicit scope, so plain text served or attached is labelled `text/plain` with no
      import.
  entry .nominable   anything `Nominable` is `Media`, by the extension of its name
      The given which gives a named thing — a file, a path, an asset — a media type guessed
      from the text after the last `.` of its name, through `Extensions.guess`:
      `report.pdf` is `application/pdf`, and a name with an unknown or no extension is
      `application/octet-stream`.
  entry .Group   the top-level type: one of exactly eleven
    detail
        `Application`, `Audio`, `Image`, `Message`, `Multipart`, `Text`, `Video`, `Font`,
        `Example`, `Model` and `Haptics` — the top-level types IANA registers, with `Haptics`
        the newest (RFC 9695). `#name` is the lower-case form written in a media type, and a
        group shows and inspects as that (`.showable`, `.inspectable`).
        Text with any other group fails to parse with `MediaType.Error.Reason.InvalidGroup`.
    entry .inspectable   a debug rendering as the group in lower case: `application`
    entry .showable   renders a group as written in a media type: `application`
    entry #name   the group in lower case, as written in a media type
  entry .Subtype   a subtype, and which of the four registration trees it is in
    detail
        `Standard(value)` is a subtype in the standards tree, registered with IANA
        (`json`, `html`); `Vendor(value)` is `vnd.value`, a vendor's own; `Personal(value)` is
        `prs.value`, a person's own; and `X(value)` is `x-value`, the old experimental prefix
        (`x.value` parses to the same). The tree is part of the value, so a match on
        `Media.Subtype.Vendor(_)` finds vendor types without inspecting a prefix. `#name`
        renders the subtype with its prefix, and the parser accepts each prefix in turn.
    entry .showable   renders a subtype as written: `vnd.api`, `prs.thing`, `x-tar`
    entry #name   the subtype with its tree's prefix, as written in a media type
  entry .Suffix   a structured syntax suffix: `+xml`, `+json`, `+zip`, …
    detail
        The suffixes IANA registers: `Xml`, `Json`, `Ber`, `Cbor`, `Der`, `FastInfoset`,
        `Wbxml`, `Zip`, `Tlv`, `JsonSeq`, `Sqlite3`, `Jwt`, `Gzip`, `CborSeq` and `Zstd`. A
        media type may carry several, in order (`application/thing+xml+gzip`). `#name` is the
        kebab-case form written in a media type — `json-seq`, `fast-infoset` — and the parser
        reads that form back case-insensitively; a suffix not in this list fails to parse with
        `MediaType.Error.Reason.InvalidSuffix`.
    entry .showable   renders a suffix as written in a media type: `json-seq`, not `JsonSeq`
    entry #name   the suffix in kebab case, as written after the `+`
  entry .unsafeParse   parses a literal the `media"…"` macro has already checked
      For the interpolator's expansion, which re-parses at runtime text verified at compile
      time, and so takes the `Unsafe` token in place of a tactic. Not for application code:
      use `as[MediaType]` or `MediaType.unapply`.
  entry .parse   parses text as a media type, raising `MediaType.Error` for what is wrong
      The parser behind `as[MediaType]`, `MediaType.unapply` and `media"…"`. It splits on
      `;` for parameters (trimming whitespace), on `/` for the group, and on `+` for suffixes;
      the group must be a `Media.Group` (case-insensitively), each suffix a `Media.Suffix` by
      its kebab-case name, and each parameter a `key=value`. Needs a `Tactic[MediaType.Error]`.
      A `MissingParam` or `NotOneSlash` fault is *raised* rather than aborting, so under a
      tolerant tactic the parse continues with a best guess (`text` as the group, the
      parameter dropped); the others abort.
  entry #mediaType   the media type of a value whose type `is Media`
      The extension every `Media` instance provides: `t"hello".mediaType`,
      `path.mediaType`, `json.mediaType`.

entry .ascribe   `value.ascribe(mediaType)`: any byte source paired with an explicit media type, as a `Content`
    An extension on any value which `is Streamable by Data over Credit` — a `Data`, a file, a
    stream — whose bytes are read as a `Chain[Data]` and paired with the given media type.
    For bytes with no intrinsic media type, where `Media` would have nothing to go on. Needs
    a `Buffering` in scope.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val data: Data = t"raw bytes".in[Data]
    val content: Content = data.ascribe(media"application/octet-stream")
    content.media             // media"application/octet-stream"
    content.named(t"raw.bin")   // an Asset
    ```

entry .Content   bytes together with their media type
  detail
      A `Content` is a `#stream` of bytes and the `#media` type which says what they mean:
      what `ascribe` produces, and what an HTTP response or an attachment is built from when
      the bytes carry no media type of their own. `#named` adds a name, making an `Asset`.
  refer gesticulate.ascribe
  refer gesticulate.Asset
  entry #media   the media type of the bytes
  entry #stream   the bytes, as a chain of chunks
  entry #named   the content with a name, as an `Asset`

entry .Asset   a named stream of bytes with its media type: an attachment, a served file
  detail
      An `Asset` is a `Content` with a `#name`: the three things an email attachment or a
      file in a multipart upload needs. `Asset(name, value)` makes one from any value which
      `is Media` and `is Streamable by Data over Credit`, taking the media type from the
      value's own `Media` instance; `Content#named` makes one from explicit content.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val asset: Asset = Asset(t"notes.txt", t"some text")
      asset.media   // media"text/plain", from `Media.text`
      ```
  refer gesticulate.Content
  entry .apply   an asset from a name and any value which `is Media` and is streamable as bytes
      The media type comes from the value's `Media` instance and the bytes from its
      `Streamable`, read whole into a chain of chunks. For bytes with no `Media` of their own, use
      `data.ascribe(mediaType).named(name)` instead.
  entry #name   the asset's name, such as a filename
  entry #media   the asset's media type
  entry #stream   the asset's bytes, as a chain of chunks

entry .Extensions   the media types of file extensions, for guessing from a filename
  detail
      A table of some eighty common extensions — `png`, `json`, `pdf`, `docx`, `woff2`, … —
      and the media type each conventionally means, with `.guess` for the lookup. It is what
      `Media.nominable` uses to label a file by its name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Extensions.guess(t"png")      // media"image/png"
      Extensions.guess(t"wobble")   // media"application/octet-stream"
      ```
  refer gesticulate.Media
  entry .guess   the media type for an extension, or `application/octet-stream` for an unknown one
      The extension is given without its dot, in lower case: `t"svg"`, not `t".SVG"`.
  entry .mediaTypes   the table itself, from extension to media type
      A `Map[Text, MediaType]`, for a program which wants to add to it or enumerate it.

entry .Multipart   a `multipart/form-data` body parsed into its parts, each lent from the input as it is read
  detail
      The format of a file upload: a body of parts separated by a boundary line, each with
      its own headers — a `Content-Disposition` giving the field's name and filename, perhaps
      a `Content-Type` — and a body. `Multipart.parse` reads one incrementally from any
      source of bytes, so a large upload streams rather than accumulating, and gives a
      `Multipart` whose `#parts` are a lazy `Chain[Part]`; `#at` finds a part by its field
      name.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A body begins with the boundary line
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val body =
              t"--xyz\r\n"
      frame
        label A part has headers, a blank line and a body, and the boundary line follows it
        code
            val body =
              t"--xyz\r\n" +
              t"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"avatar\"; filename=\"me.png\"\r\n" +
              t"Content-Type: image/png\r\n" +
              t"\r\n" +
              t"pixels\r\n" +
              t"--xyz\r\n"
      frame
        label A second part, and the closing boundary with its trailing `--`
        code
            val body =
              t"--xyz\r\n" +
              t"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"avatar\"; filename=\"me.png\"\r\n" +
              t"Content-Type: image/png\r\n" +
              t"\r\n" +
              t"pixels\r\n" +
              t"--xyz\r\n" +
              t"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"caption\"\r\n" +
              t"\r\n" +
              t"Me, at the beach\r\n" +
              t"--xyz--\r\n"
      frame
        label Parsed, the parts are typed
        code
            val upload = Multipart.parse(Chain(body.in[Data]))
            upload.at(t"avatar").let(_.filename)                 // t"me.png"
            upload.at(t"avatar").let(_.headers.at(t"Content-Type"))   // t"image/png"
            upload.parts.map(_.read[Data].utf8)                  // Chain(t"pixels", t"Me, at the beach")
      ##
      ```

      A part's body is **lent** from the input, zero-copy, for as long as that part is the
      current one: read it (`part.read[Data]`, or `part.body()` for the stream) before moving
      on to the next part, because forcing the next part skips whatever remained of this one,
      after which it reads as empty. A `Chain` memoises, so `parts.size` followed by
      `parts.head.let(_.read[Data])` reads nothing. Bodies are found by a bulk search for the
      delimiter, so a 100 KB binary body arrives as a few large regions whatever the chunking
      of the input, and a body may be read in pieces, each `body()` continuing where the last
      stopped.

      A malformed body raises `Multipart.Error` with a `.Error.Reason`; scintillate's
      `Acceptable.multipart` reads a request's `Content-Type` for the boundary and parses the
      request body with this.
  refer gesticulate.Multipart.Part
  refer gesticulate.MediaType
  entry .Disposition   how a part is meant to be presented: `Inline`, `Attachment` or `FormData`
      The token of the part's `Content-Disposition` header. A form upload's parts are
      `FormData`; `Inline` and `Attachment` are the dispositions of a message's parts. A
      token other than these fails the parse with `Multipart.Error.Reason.BadDisposition`.
  entry .parse   parses a multipart body from any source of bytes, lending each part as it is reached
      `Multipart.parse(input)` takes any value which `is Streamable by Data over Credit` — a
      `Chain[Data]`, a request body, a file — and reads the boundary from its first line
      (`--boundary`), so the boundary need not be known in advance; the second parameter,
      `boundary`, is accepted for a caller which has it from a `Content-Type` header but is
      not consulted. It needs a `Tactic[Multipart.Error]`, which the result retains (a part's
      body raises a cut-short read through it), and raises `Multipart.Error` at the first
      malformed byte: `Expected(char)` for a boundary or line ending that is not there,
      `BadDisposition` for an unreadable `Content-Disposition`. Headers are read as ASCII; a
      part with no `Content-Disposition` is `FormData` with no name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      capture[Multipart.Error](Multipart.parse(Chain[Data]())).reason   // Expected('-')
      ```
  entry .Error   raised when a multipart body cannot be read
    detail
        Error 937: _multipart data could not be read because `reason`_. Raised through a
        `Tactic[Multipart.Error]` by `Multipart.parse` as it reaches the fault, which, since
        the parts are read lazily, may be while a later part is forced rather than at the
        call. The `.Reason`'s number is the error's sub-number (937.1 to 937.6).
    entry .Reason   why a multipart body could not be read
      detail
          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Expected(char)`  1  _the character '`char`' was expected_  a boundary without its `--`, a header line or a boundary line without its `\r\n`, a header without `: `
          row  `StreamContinues`  2  _the stream continues beyond the last part_
          row  `BadBoundaryEnding`  3  _unexpected content followed the boundary_
          row  `MediaType`  4  _the media type is invalid_  raised by scintillate's `Acceptable.multipart` for a request whose `Content-Type` is not `multipart/form-data` with a `boundary`
          row  `BadDisposition`  5  _the `Content-Disposition` header has the wrong format_
          row  `Truncated`  6  _the body ended before its closing boundary_  which `Acceptable.multipart` raises for a request body cut short
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's number, `1` to `6`, which is the error's sub-number
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   why the body could not be read
  entry #parts   the parts, in order, as a lazy chain
      Each part is parsed as the chain is forced, and forcing a part's successor skips and
      closes the part's body. The chain memoises, so it may be traversed more than once, but
      a body may be read only while its part is the latest forced.
  entry #at   the part with a field name, or `Unset`
      `upload.at(t"avatar")` finds the first part whose `Content-Disposition` names the
      field. Finding it forces every part up to it, and so closes the bodies of the parts
      before it.

  entry .Part   one part of a multipart body: its disposition, headers, name, filename and lent body
    detail
        A `Part` is what `Multipart.parse` yields for each section of the body: the
        `#disposition` and the `#name` and `#filename` parameters from its `Content-Disposition`
        header, all of its `#headers` by name, and its `#body`, a `Spring[Data]` lent from the
        input. A part `is Streamable by Data over Credit` (`.streamable`), so its body is read
        with the usual verbs — `part.read[Data]`, `part.read[Text]` with a `Charset` in scope,
        or `part.body()` for the stream itself.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val body =
          t"--xyz\r\n" +
          t"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"file\"; filename=\"hello.txt\"\r\n" +
          t"Content-Type: text/plain\r\n" +
          t"\r\n" +
          t"file content\r\n" +
          t"--xyz--\r\n"

        val part: Optional[Multipart.Part] = Multipart.parse(Chain(body.in[Data])).parts.head
        part.let(_.filename)                     // t"hello.txt"
        part.let(_.disposition)                  // Multipart.Disposition.FormData
        part.let(_.headers.at(t"Content-Type"))  // t"text/plain"
        part.let(_.read[Data].utf8)              // t"file content"
        ```

        The body is zero-copy: it is readable until the next part is forced, which skips
        whatever remains of it, after which it reads as empty. Each `body()` continues from
        wherever the previous read stopped, so a body may be taken in two pieces, but it cannot
        be read twice: a part whose body is wanted later must be read into a `Data` first.
    refer gesticulate.Multipart
    entry .streamable   `Part is Streamable by Data over Credit`: a part's body, for `read[Data]` and the other stream verbs
        In implicit scope. It needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, which the instance retains,
        for a body whose input ends before the closing boundary.
    entry #disposition   `Inline`, `Attachment` or `FormData`, or `Unset` if the part had no `Content-Disposition`
    entry #headers   every header of the part, by name as written
        Header names are matched exactly as they appear in the body, `Content-Type` and not
        `content-type`.
    entry #name   the field name from the `Content-Disposition` header, or `Unset`
    entry #filename   the filename from the `Content-Disposition` header, or `Unset`
        Present for a file field of a form; absent for a text field.
    entry #body   the part's bytes, lent from the input: `body()` is the stream
        A `Spring[Data]`: `body()` gives the `Stream[Data] over Credit` from wherever the
        previous read stopped, and needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`. It reads as empty once
        the next part has been forced.

entry .Construable   the Scala type a media type is read as and written from, for an HTTP client's use
  detail
      `("image/png" is Construable to (Raster in Png))` says that a body of `image/png` is
      read as, and written from, a `Raster in Png`. The `Self` is the media type without its
      parameters, as a singleton string type, so that a client which learns a media type from
      a specification — an OpenAPI document, say — can ask, as the code compiles, what to
      construe a response of that type as. The trait itself carries no operations: the result
      type's own `Aggregable` reads the body and its `Postable` writes one. `Construable()`
      makes an instance, with the two types as arguments.
      Its `Result`, the Scala type a body of that media type is read as and written from, is
      inherited from `prepositional.Resultant`.

      Each mapping is a given in the `construables` choice package, imported by name, so
      nothing is construed without a deliberate choice: this library declares
      `plainTextConstruable` and `octetStreamConstruable`, and each format library declares
      its own — `jsonConstruable` in jacinta's HTTP module, `xmlConstruable` and
      `textXmlConstruable` in xylophone's, `pngConstruable` in hallucination's, and so on —
      all merged into the umbrella's `construables` package. Without the right one in scope, a
      client which reads responses by their `Content-Type` falls back to the raw body.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import construables.plainTextConstruable

      summon["text/plain" is Construable to Text]
      ```
  refer gesticulate.construables
  refer gesticulate.MediaType
  entry .Bound   a `Construable` with its media type and result fixed as type arguments
    detail
        `Construable.Bound[media, result]` is the class `Construable()` instantiates: a
        `Construable` whose `Self` is `media` and `Result` is `result`.
    entry #Self   the media type, as a singleton string
    entry #Result   the Scala type the media type is construed as
  entry .apply   `Construable[media, result]()`: a mapping from a media type literal to a type
      Written with the `is … to …` syntax as a given's type, the type arguments are inferred:
      `given csvConstruable: ("text/csv" is Construable to Sheet) = Construable()`.
  entry #Self   the media type, without parameters, as a singleton string type

entry .construables   the mappings from media types to Scala types, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which type a body of some media type is construed as is a `Construable` given in scope,
      and `import soundness.*` brings none of these in: each is imported by name. This package
      holds the two mappings which need no format library, and the umbrella's `construables`
      package merges them with every format library's own.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import construables.{plainTextConstruable, octetStreamConstruable}
      ```
  entry .plainTextConstruable   `text/plain` is construed as `Text`
      Reading the body as text needs a `Charset` in scope, and writing one a `Codepage`.
  entry .octetStreamConstruable   `application/octet-stream` is construed as `Data`

entry .internal   the macro behind `media"…"` and its registry check
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro has a stable home, and is kept
      self-contained so that it can run at expansion time.
  entry .validateLiteral   checks a media type literal's group, subtype characters and registration, as a message or `Unset`
      The compile-time check: the group must be one of the eleven, the subtype's segments
      must contain no forbidden character, and a standard-tree subtype must be in the
      registry, else a message naming the nearest registered type by Levenshtein distance.
      Suffixes and parameters are left to the parser.
  entry .mediaInterpolator   the macro: validates the one literal part and splices the parsed media type
      Halts for a literal with substitutions, for a `validateLiteral` message, or for a
      `MediaType.Error` from the parser.
